
Ex4 Part 1
The two main functions in my program is convertImageToAscii which is private and
chooseChars which is public. in chooseChars, I initialise an array containing the
brightnesses of the letters (Characters) we have using a private method that passes through
all the letters, on each one it calculates its brightness by counting the number of trues
in its boolean representation. Then I do a linearStretching for these brightnesses values
using an equation given. So now , we have an array of letters and an array of brightnesses,
such that the brightness at index i is the brightness of the letter at index i in the array 
of letters, so I didn't sort the array of brightnesses. Then I called convertImageToAscii method
that takes these two arrays and numCharsInRow. It cuts the image to subImages, and on each
subImage, I calculate its average brightness, and then I find the letter with the closest
brightness to it by this way: I did a loop on the array of brightnesses each time
checking for smaller difference, and then I find the smallest difference between a brightness
and the subImage, so that, if the brightness causing the smallest difference is at index i,
then the letter matching this brightness is at index i in the array of letters.
then I add this letter in the right place in the asciiArt two-dimensional array 
using relevant indexes. Then I return this array.

EX4 part 2

In the BrightnessImgCharMatcher class, I did the same thing as in Part 1, but I added
a hashmap that can store the brightnesses of the subImages, so that if we want to
convert the image to an asciiArt one more time, we don't need to calculate the brightness
of each subImage again.
In the shell I used Hashset to store the letters we have, because in HashSet there are
no duplicates, and removing a letter from it and checking if a letter in it takes O(1).

findDuplicate algorithm:
 1) Correctness:
  Each new element in the sequence is an element in numList at the index of the previous
  element. So, starting from numList[0], a linked list with a cycle is produced. There is 
  cycle because numList contains duplicate element and this element is the start of the cycle.
  so the algorithm uses two pointer, one that is faster than the other. the faster enters the 
  cycle first, and then the slower, and since it is moving slower they will intersect at some 
  point. now move the slowPointer to the starting point of the sequence and the faster remains
  within the cycle, and move both with the same speed, they will intersect in the cycle entrance
  which is the duplicate.
 2) Time complexity:
  n = numList.length. In the first loop we have two pointer, one faster than the other.
  They move around a cycle with length of maximum n. speed of faster pointer = 2 * slower one,
  so for sure the number of steps they will do until intersection is O(n). In the second loop,
  we have two pointer moving at the same speed until they intersect, so for sure this is O(n),
  so time Complexity = 2O(n) = O(n) (because all other things are O(1)).

uniqueMorseRepresentations algorithm:
 1) Correctness:
  I initialized a HashSet.I moved using a loop on the array of words we have. For each word,I build
  a morse word that matches it. the letters a-z has asciiValues starts from 97-122, so to get
  the morse letter that matches a char i did morseTable[charAscii - 97]. then I add the morse
  word to the Set. if two identical morse words are added to the set, they ara considered as one
  because there is no repetition in Sets. so at the end Set.size() will be the number of unique Morse
  words that are in the array of words.
 2) Time complexity:
  n = words.length.
  I build the morseTable then initialized a HashSet. Then I did a loop that passes through
  the arrays of words (loop of length n), for every iteration, I initialized a
  StringBuilder, and then I did a loop that passes through the letters in each word (loop of
  length = length of the word), each time appending a morse letter to the StringBuilder (append
  takes constant time). and then I added the morse word to the Set (O(1)). So we have two loops,
  one of length n (number of words we have) and inside it a loop with a length equals to the 
  length of the word. so the time complexity = O(number of words * length of each word).
   



